/* Clip Review content pack: Apex Legends. The app engine reads window.PACK; a different game is a different pack file. */
window.PACK = {"id":"apex","storagePrefix":"apex","game":"Apex Legends","version":"2026.11b","modules":{"0":"Foundations · Tempo & Advantage","1":"Ring & Zone Control","2":"Third Party Awareness","3":"Fight or Flight Decisions","4":"Gunfight Fundamentals","5":"Comms & Teamplay","6":"Mindset & Review","7":"Spacing & Space Control"},"moduleWhy":{"0":"You're taking fights without an edge. Study tempo and advantage (Module 0).","1":"You're getting caught by the zone. Study rotations and zone calling (Module 1).","2":"Fights collapse on you. Study third-party awareness (Module 2).","3":"Pushes are costing runs. Study when to swing and when to reset (Module 3).","4":"You reach fights and lose them. Study peeking, crosshair and movement (Module 4).","5":"Your team is out of sync. Study comms and teamplay (Module 5).","7":"You're stacking or giving up space. Study spacing (Module 7)."},"cards":[{"id":"0.1","mod":0,"title":"Tempo is who controls the pace","roles":["All"],"concept":"Tempo is who's making the decisions versus who's reacting, and it shifts like tides. Positive tempo: you got a knock, forced a heal, rotated early, so you choose what happens next. Negative tempo: you're cracked, healing, answering their moves. Read it live with one question on repeat: who's deciding right now, me or them? The moment the answer is “them,” make a move that forces them to answer you.","recall":"What does having tempo mean, and what's the one question that reads it live?","drill":"Mid-fight, ask “who's deciding right now?” The instant it's “them,” make one move that forces them to react to you.","absorbs":["0.1d"],"kp":[["Tempo = who's making the decisions vs. who's reacting",["decid","decision","dictat","control pace","control the pace","initiative","react","call the shots","in control"]],["The live question: who's deciding right now, me or them?",["who decid","who decis","me or them","us or them","who control","who is in control","whos in control","who has control"]]]},{"id":"0.1a","mod":0,"title":"Tempo · taking it in a fight","roles":["All"],"concept":"Inside a fight, tempo is bought with actions that force a reaction: a knock (they're a player down), a crack (they have to back off or heal), a forced heal, or burned utility. Each one puts them on the clock and hands you the initiative. You don't wait for tempo to show up. You take an action that leaves them no choice but to answer, then stack those forced reactions until they're always a step behind.","recall":"Name the in-fight actions that buy you tempo, and what they all have in common.","drill":"In each fight, name the moment you took tempo (“knock, tempo”). Notice when you had it and didn't press it.","absorbs":["0.1c"],"kp":[["A knock or a crack",["knock","crack","down","damage","break shield","shield break","broke"]],["Forcing a heal or burning their utility",["heal","util","abilit","nade","cooldown","ult"]],["They all force a reaction and put them on the clock",["force","react","answer","clock","respond","initiative","no choice","on the back foot","behind"]]]},{"id":"0.1b","mod":0,"title":"Tempo · outside fights","roles":["Alter","Valk"],"concept":"Most of your tempo is won or lost before a shot. Rotate early and you get to set up, scout, and hold space while the lobby reacts to you. Rotate late and you're behind the clock, forced through the open and into set-up teams. Be the faster team: faster loot, faster rotates, faster decisions. The ring is a tempo engine: it either works for you (you're already where it's pulling) or it forces your hand.","recall":"How do you gain or lose tempo without firing a shot?","drill":"One game, commit to rotating a beat earlier than feels natural. Notice how much more the game bends to you when you arrive first.","kp":[["Rotate early (rotate late and you lose it)",["early","late","ahead","before the ring","before ring","first"]],["Be the faster team: set up first, loot and decide faster",["fast","quick","speed","decision","decid","loot","set up","setup","scout","hold space","take space"]],["Use the ring: it works for you or forces your hand",["ring","zone","circle","storm","clock","timer"]]]},{"id":"0.2","mod":0,"title":"Don't waste tempo","roles":["All"],"concept":"When you're ahead on time — cleared your side, won a fight, rotated early — the mistake is doing nothing with it, or forcing a greedy extra kill. Spend won time on getting stronger (loot/upgrade) or getting ahead (take a power position). A pro in these videos cleared his side, had 15s to see the next zone, greedily pushed two teams instead, and nearly threw the game.","recall":"Last game, did you ever hold free time and waste it chasing one more kill instead of rotating or resetting?","drill":"The moment you win a fight, before anything else ask: “what does this free time buy — position or gear?” Then do that, not a greedy third.","selfGrade":true},{"id":"0.3","mod":0,"title":"Strong side vs weak side: never take a 50/50","roles":["All"],"concept":"Every fight starts from advantage (strong side) or disadvantage (weak side). Three things decide it: Position (height, cover, angles), Resources (armor, meds, nades, ults), and Control (who's forcing whom). A 50/50 is a fight where none of those favors you: a fair, open 1v1 decided by a coin flip on aim. Refuse it. Stack one advantage first (an angle, height, a health lead, a teammate on a second angle) until the fight isn't fair. If it's a coin flip, you set it up wrong.","recall":"Name the three things that decide strong vs weak side, and what you do when none of them favor you.","drill":"The instant you see a team, label it out loud: “strong side” or “weak side,” and why. If you can't name an advantage, don't take the fight yet.","absorbs":["3.8"],"kp":[["Position: height, cover, angles",["position","height","high ground","cover","angle","terrain"]],["Resources: armor, heals, nades, ults",["resource","armor","armour","shield","heal","med","nade","grenade","ult","util","loot","gear","health","hp"]],["Control: who's forcing whom",["control","forcing","force","tempo","initiative","pressure"]],["None favor you: refuse the 50/50 and stack an advantage first",["refuse","avoid","dont take","dont fight","skip","leave","disengage","back off","reset","stack","set up","setup","advantage","wait","no fight","coin"]]]},{"id":"0.4","mod":0,"title":"Flip weak side — don't brute-force it","roles":["Conduit","Catalyst"],"concept":"On weak side your job isn't to win the fight, it's to change it. Two priorities: stabilize (control your space, hold their pushes) and patience (wait for their mistake). Reposition, break line of sight, bait cooldowns, use utility to make space until it flips. Moving while weak is just rotating into death.","recall":"You're low ground, undergeared, getting pushed. What are your two priorities before anything else?","drill":"Next time you're weak side, resist the swing. Heal, reset, hold, wait for one enemy to overextend — then punish. Conduit and Catalyst are built for exactly this.","kp":[["Stabilize: control your space and hold their pushes",["stabil","hold","control","space","survive","stay alive","defend","cover","reposition","break line"]],["Patience: wait for their mistake",["patien","wait","mistake","bait","slow down","dont push","error"]]]},{"id":"1.1","mod":1,"title":"Rotate with a reason","roles":["Alter","Valk"],"concept":"Every rotation needs a purpose — secure space, fight for a position, gather info. Rotating “just because the ring is closing” is the number-one rotation mistake; it forces you through open ground, chokes, and set-up teams. Most fights you lose weren't unwinnable — they were avoidable.","recall":"What's the difference between rotating because the ring closed and rotating with a reason?","drill":"Before each rotation, say the reason: “moving to take the NW building for height.” No reason and you're safe? Don't move yet.","kp":[["Ring-forced rotations push you through open ground, chokes and set-up teams",["forced","open","choke","late","set up","setup","third","trap","exposed","bad position","death"]],["Rotating with a reason has a purpose: space, a position, or info",["purpose","reason","goal","plan","space","position","info","spot","intent"]]]},{"id":"1.2","mod":1,"title":"Take the path that gives options","roles":["All"],"concept":"The best path isn't the fastest — it's the one that keeps your options open. Good paths give cover, side lines and escape routes. Bad paths funnel you through open ground or chokes with no exit. Ask: “if something goes wrong here, can we adjust?”","recall":"When choosing a rotation path, what's the one question to ask about it?","drill":"On your next three rotations, deliberately pick the path with cover and an exit even if it's slower. Notice how much less you get shot.","kp":[["If something goes wrong here, can we adjust?",["option","adjust","escape","exit","back out","bail","plan b","go wrong","goes wrong","alternative"]]]},{"id":"1.3","mod":1,"title":"Zone play vs Edge play — and which YOUR legends want","roles":["Catalyst","Maggie"],"concept":"Two styles. Zone: rotate early, take a power position, control space, trade loot/KP for safety. Edge: loot up, get strong, fight your way in — fighting IS the rotation. Neither is wrong; rotating the wrong way for your style gets you killed. For you: Catalyst and Conduit want zone (hold). Maggie and Ash want edge (fight in). Alter and Valk flex and feed the info.","recall":"Which of your legends want to play zone (hold), and which want to play edge (fight in)?","drill":"Pick your legend at drop and commit to its style for the whole game. Catalyst game = play zone. Maggie game = play edge. Don't blend them.","kp":[["Zone (hold): Catalyst and Conduit",["catalyst","conduit"]],["Edge (fight in): Maggie and Ash",["maggie","ash"]],["Alter and Valk flex",["alter","valk","flex"]]]},{"id":"1.4","mod":1,"title":"A power position is one YOUR comp can hold","roles":["Catalyst","Conduit"],"concept":"A power position gives you the edge over anyone pushing: height, cover, sightlines, room to reset. But it only counts if your comp can hold it. The same building is a fortress for a Catalyst team and a death trap for a team with no way to lock it. Judge a building before you commit: few entrances (ideally two doorways you can cover), windows that are hard to peek into, real interior cover, and a second exit. Three or more entrances on one floor, an open floor with no cover, or a dead end with no way out are traps.","recall":"What makes a spot a power position, and why can't every team hold every one?","drill":"Before holding a building, count the entrances, find interior cover, and confirm an exit. In a Catalyst game, claim the spot your walls make un-pushable.","absorbs":["1.10"],"kp":[["Gives you the edge: height, cover, sightlines, room to reset",["height","high","cover","sightline","sight line","angle","reset","edge","advantage","elevat"]],["Your comp has to be able to hold it",["comp","legend","abilit","team","squad","catalyst","lock","hold it","kit"]],["Few entrances and a second exit",["entrance","door","exit","entry","ways in","way in","window"]]]},{"id":"1.5","mod":1,"title":"Avoid the heavy side and the center","roles":["All"],"concept":"Every map has a natural flow: teams move along predictable routes set by POIs, terrain, chokes and where the zone pulls. The heavy side is where more teams collide, which means forced fights and third parties. The center POI is the heavy side almost every game: teams arrive from every direction and its chokes are tight, so a slow rotate there gets you held and thirded. Rotate through low ground, buildings and dead space on the quiet side. If teams see you rotate, you're a target, not a threat.","recall":"What's the heavy side, why do you avoid it, and how do you rotate to stay unseen?","drill":"Open the map, guess where the lobby is (usually the center) and plan your route through the quiet side. If you must cross the center, do it fast with cover.","absorbs":["1.11"],"kp":[["Heavy side: where more teams collide",["more teams","most teams","collide","busy","crowded","traffic","everyone","lots of teams","many teams","flow"]],["You get forced fights and third parties there",["third","forced","force","pinch","sandwich"]],["Rotate quiet: low ground, buildings, dead space",["low ground","building","dead space","quiet","unseen","hidden","stealth","cover","out of sight","behind"]]]},{"id":"1.6","mod":1,"title":"Play the phase you're in","roles":["All"],"concept":"Each game phase has one job, and everything early is setup for what's next. Early: get loot / EVO / attachments however you can (unlooted areas, fair fights, ratting a leaving team, a clean third) so you're equipped for the mid game. Mid: you roughly know where endgame is — only rotate and take fights that move you toward a strong endgame position. Late: hold a spot that's hard to push into but easy to push from, and punish teams caught late in the open.","recall":"What's the one job of each phase — early, mid, late?","drill":"At each phase change, state your job out loud: “early — get EVO,” “mid — rotate to the endgame side,” “late — hold and punish.”","kp":[["Early: loot up and get equipped",["loot","gear","equip","evo","attachment","armor","armour"]],["Mid: only rotations and fights that lead to a strong endgame spot",["endgame","end game","position","toward","towards","set up","setup","spot","late game"]],["Late: hold a hard-to-push spot and punish teams in the open",["hold","punish","defend","survive","hard to push","open","caught"]]]},{"id":"1.7","mod":1,"title":"Zone calling: scan early, think two rings ahead","roles":["Alter","Valk"],"concept":"Zone calling is predicting where the final rings will pull and rotating off it. Scan the ring as early as you can: ring one tells you almost nothing, and an early scan gets you to the power spot before the lobby. Ring three's scan is the biggest info drop, because it shows whether the zone does a dramatic pullback. Confident off ring two? Commit early and take the spot uncontested. Not sure? Go to the dead center of the current ring so you're fine whichever way it shifts.","recall":"Why scan early, which scan is the biggest info drop, and what's the safe play when you're unsure?","drill":"Each game, scan the ring as early as you can and call where the next two rings pull. Unsure? Rotate to the dead center of the green.","absorbs":["1.9"],"kp":[["Early scans get you to the power spot before the lobby",["before the lobby","before others","before everyone","first","early","beat","uncontested","ahead"]],["Ring three's scan is the biggest info drop",["three","3","third","r3"]],["Unsure? Go to the dead center of the current ring",["center","centre","middle"]]]},{"id":"1.8","mod":1,"title":"How the zone algorithm works","roles":["All"],"concept":"Respawn confirmed it: when the ring shifts it tries to center itself, but it can never end in unplayable space. So read where the current ring and the next ring touch. If they touch at an unplayable edge (water, the map's hard edge), expect the zone to keep pulling INTO that edge — it can't re-center past dead space, so it hugs it. If they touch inside playable area, expect the next ring to re-center inside the current one instead of continuing the pull. The touch-point tells you whether the pull keeps going or resets.","recall":"Rings touch at a hard edge vs. in playable space — what does each tell you about the next pull?","drill":"Each ring, check where the current and next ring touch and whether it's playable or unplayable. Call the likely pull before the scan confirms it.","kp":[["Touching at a hard edge: the pull keeps going into that edge",["keep pull","keeps pulling","continue","hug","into the edge","into that edge","same direction","keep going","toward the edge","towards the edge","pull into","pulls into","keeps going"]],["Touching in playable space: the next ring re-centers",["recenter","recentre","center","centre","middle","reset","pull back","pullback"]]]},{"id":"2.1","mod":2,"title":"Be the outside team; force teams into each other","roles":["Alter","Valk"],"concept":"With good info, force teams to fight each other and play the outside — third-party IN rather than getting sandwiched in the middle. Pressure one team so it pushes into another, then clean up from the edge. That's controlling the game instead of being caught between two fights.","recall":"What's the difference between third-partying a fight and getting sandwiched — and how do you stay on the outside?","drill":"When you spot two teams near each other, don't rush between them. Position to pressure one INTO the other, then clean the outside.","kp":[["Third-partying hits from outside; sandwiched means caught between two teams",["outside","edge","middle","between","sandwich","pinch","caught"]],["Stay outside: use info and push teams into each other",["info","force","into each other","each other","pressure","let them fight","wait"]]]},{"id":"2.2","mod":2,"title":"Anticipate the third before it lands","roles":["All"],"concept":"Read the tells. A team gets an opening knock but doesn't push across? There's probably another team nearby they're wary of. Some POIs are third-party central — expect angles from everywhere there. Don't get surprised; pre-plan your exit before you commit.","recall":"A team knocks one of your squad but doesn't push in. What does that most likely tell you?","drill":"The moment a fight starts, ask “where's the third coming from?” and pre-pick the cover that protects that angle.","kp":[["There's probably another team nearby they're wary of",["another team","other team","third team","third party","second team","someone else","another squad","other squad","team nearby","nearby team","wary","worried","scared","afraid"]]]},{"id":"2.3","mod":2,"title":"Clear your back","roles":["All"],"concept":"Wiping a team behind you isn't only KP — it's safety. Clear the squad that could rotate in behind you and you get to play your position without being shot in the back. Sometimes you take a fight purely to clear your back.","recall":"Give one reason to take a fight even when it isn't for free KP.","drill":"Before committing forward, physically check your back — especially while healing. The players in these videos do it every single fight.","kp":[["To clear your back, so no one can rotate in behind you",["clear your back","clear behind","behind you","behind us","safety","safe","no one behind","rotate in behind","cover your back","watch your back","team behind","squad behind","back is clear","clear our back"]]]},{"id":"2.4","mod":2,"title":"Timing beats speed on a third","roles":["Alter","Maggie"],"concept":"Show up too early and you get pinched. Too late and you're cleaning scraps — and might die to whoever won. On time, you flow in behind the chaos and take it. Good teams rotate in when the other squads are already committed to each other.","recall":"Why is arriving “on time” to a third party better than arriving fast?","drill":"When two teams start fighting, wait until they're committed and weakened, then move — don't sprint in at the first shot.","kp":[["Too early and you get pinched",["early","pinch","sandwich","both teams","turn on you"]],["Too late and you get scraps, or die to the winner",["late","scrap","winner","healed","already over","too slow"]],["On time, they're committed to each other and you flow in behind",["committed","busy","distract","chaos","mid fight","weak","each other","focused"]]]},{"id":"3.1","mod":3,"title":"The reset: fall back and get info","roles":["Alter"],"concept":"A hard push over open space usually isn't worth it. Falling back to reset and grabbing a recon scan is often the stronger play. Information is king — enemy positions, zone, anything makes your next decision sharper. A reset isn't passive; it's setting up a better fight.","recall":"When a push looks hard — open space, strong enemy spot — what's often the better play?","drill":"Next time a push feels forced, call “reset.” Fall back to cover, get a scan or break line of sight, re-approach with a plan. Alter gives you the scan for free.","kp":[["Fall back and reset",["fall back","back","reset","retreat","disengage","pull back","leave","back off"]],["Get info (a recon scan)",["info","scan","recon","intel","beacon","survey","scout"]]]},{"id":"3.2","mod":3,"title":"Don't overcommit: cut your losses","roles":["All"],"concept":"Tunnel-visioning one fight can drop you from 1st to 6th. Heal fully, let teammates capitalize off your damage, and prioritize staying up over one more kill: first place is worth far more RP than a couple of extra elims. Apex is a dance, not a marriage. When a fight starts slipping, drop the ego and make space to survive instead of forcing it to the death. Cutting a loss is a win.","recall":"What's the real cost of overcommitting late, and what do you do when a fight starts slipping?","drill":"Late game, before every aggressive move ask: “does this risk my life for something that isn't winning?” If a fight is slipping, disengage and reset.","absorbs":["6.2"],"kp":[["Overcommitting can drop you from 1st to 6th; placement is worth more RP",["placement","rp","place","1st","first","6th","sixth","points","die","dying"]],["When it slips: drop the ego, make space, survive",["survive","stay alive","ego","leave","back off","reset","disengage","make space","cut","retreat","get out"]]]},{"id":"3.3","mod":3,"title":"Play your teammates' pace, and pinch, never stack","roles":["All"],"concept":"As a solo, play your teammates' pace, not your own. If they're behind, don't push in alone: scout, get info, let them catch up, and keep them up first. When you do fight the same team, never run the same angle. Split to a different one and create a pinch. Even if the enemy focuses your teammate, you're dealing free damage from the side, and cover can't protect them from two directions at once.","recall":"Teammates are behind: what do you do? And when you fight together, what's the angle rule?","drill":"Every team fight, split to a different angle than your teammate. This is the highest-value habit for your solo-queue win rate.","absorbs":["4.6"],"kp":[["Teammates behind: don't push alone; scout and let them catch up",["catch up","wait","slow","their pace","dont push","scout","info","stay with","alone"]],["Angle rule: never stack, split to a different angle for a pinch",["pinch","different angle","another angle","other angle","split","not the same","never the same","stack","spread","flank","crossfire","cross fire"]]]},{"id":"3.4","mod":3,"title":"Think in chess: use teammate abilities","roles":["Conduit","Catalyst"],"concept":"Stop running on autopilot. Before and during fights ask: “what abilities do my teammates have, and what spots can we play with them?” Realizing you'll need a specific ult to hold a spot — and planning around it — turns the game into chess: thinking steps ahead with your pieces instead of reacting.","recall":"What single question turns autopilot into chess-thinking?","drill":"At each game's start, note your squad's key ults (yours plus both teammates). Plan one late-game spot you could hold using them.","kp":[["What abilities do my teammates have, and what spots can we play with them?",["abilit","ult","kit","util"]]]},{"id":"3.5","mod":3,"title":"One small play, then back up, scaled to the damage","roles":["All"],"concept":"You win fights with a chain of small plays, not one big commitment. Swing in, do one thing (damage or a knock), then back up before you take too much, even if you're winning. Small play, reset, cell, small play again. Let the damage you just did decide how hard you go: crack someone for 150 and you can holster and sprint in; chip them for 20 and pushing gets you reset and knocked. It works squad-wide too: when the health trade tips against your team, all three of you back up and heal together.","recall":"Why back up after a small play even when winning, and what tells you to push hard versus reset as a squad?","drill":"Every fight: swing, do one thing, back up, heal on the move. Push hard only off big damage. If your squad is losing the trade, call the reset.","absorbs":["3.9"],"kp":[["Back up so you don't take too much damage, then reset and cell",["damage","trade","too much","heal","cell","reset","shield","safe","punish"]],["The damage decides: big crack, push; small chip, reset",["crack","how much","150","chip","health","lead","hp","big","damage"]],["Squad-wide: when the trade turns, all back up together",["together","squad","team","all three","everyone","all of us"]]]},{"id":"3.6","mod":3,"title":"Make the opportunity: force them passive, then close","roles":["Maggie","Ash"],"concept":"Don't wait for a knock; make it happen. At range you can't control knocks (they just duck behind cover), so your job there is to force the enemy passive: nade them off an angle, pre-fire their corner, smoke them, or hold their angle so hard they can't peek. While they can't fight back, they can't stop you walking up, so close the gap and swing, where you can lock them into a fight. Deny their best option, then step up.","recall":"At range you can't reliably get a knock. What's your actual job before you push?","drill":"Next range standoff, use utility or a hard hold to force them passive, then immediately close distance and swing.","absorbs":["4.11"],"kp":[["Force them passive (nades, pre-fire, smoke, hold their angle)",["passive","force","pin","suppress","pressure","nade","prefire","pre fire","smoke","hold their angle","deny"]],["Then close the gap and swing",["close","walk up","push","gap","move up","swing","get close","approach"]]]},{"id":"3.7","mod":3,"title":"Only do what you'll play off of","roles":["All"],"concept":"Don't shoot at things you won't push. Poking a far team you won't commit to wastes bullets and heals, tells the whole lobby where you are (inviting thirds), and pulls your attention off teams that can actually hurt you. If you're not going to play off an action, don't take it. Same with abilities — don't poke and eat damage right before the teammate ult push. Execute the plan; don't split your attention.","recall":"Before you take a shot or a poke, what should you ask yourself?","drill":"Each time you're about to shoot a far team, ask “am I going to push this?” If no, hold fire and watch your flanks instead.","kp":[["Am I actually going to play off this?",["play off","follow up","follow through","going to push","will i push","am i going","actually push","commit to","worth it","going to use","going to follow"]]]},{"id":"4.1","mod":4,"title":"Cover, sightlines, spacing","roles":["All"],"concept":"Three elements make in-fight positioning work. Cover: not just hiding — controlling how much of you they see while you can still shoot, so you can poke and reposition safely. Sightlines: control what you can see vs what they can, so you react early. Spacing: distance from enemies (dodge nades/flanks), teammates (support), and zone. Together they win the fight before a shot.","recall":"Name the three positioning elements and what each one does for you.","drill":"Every fight, pick cover you can both shoot from AND retreat behind. If it can't do both, it's bad cover — move.","kp":[["Cover: controls how much of you they see",["cover"]],["Sightlines: what you can see vs. what they can",["sightline","sight line","sight","vision","see","line of sight","angle"]],["Spacing: distance from enemies, teammates and zone",["spacing","space","distance","spread"]]]},{"id":"4.2","mod":4,"title":"Peek small, re-peek from a new angle","roles":["All"],"concept":"Wide-peeking shows your full body. Hug geometry and peek narrow: you see more of them and they hit less of you. Save the wide swing for when you've cracked someone and want to secure the knock. Once you've peeked a spot, they know where you are, so re-peek from a different angle and catch them adjusting. Never fire from the same window twice in a row.","recall":"When is a wide swing correct, and why never peek the same spot twice?","drill":"Peek narrow, then move after every peek. Only wide-swing after you've cracked someone.","absorbs":["4.3"],"kp":[["Wide swing only after a crack, to secure the knock",["crack","finish","secure","low","knock","damage","weak","broken"]],["Peek the same spot twice and they're waiting; re-peek from a new angle",["know where","expect","preaim","pre aim","prefire","pre fire","waiting","holding","predict","new angle","different angle","another angle","other angle","same spot"]]]},{"id":"4.4","mod":4,"title":"Pre-aim where they'll appear; don't flick","roles":["All"],"concept":"Players hug geometry, so they peek from a small set of spots: the door, either side of a pillar, the roof, a box edge. Hold your crosshair on the spot you expect and pre-fire. A flick is a correction: it means you guessed wrong and are fixing it under pressure. If your crosshair is already where they appear, you just pull the trigger, and you beat anyone who has to flick to you.","recall":"Instead of racing your aim, what actually wins a peek battle?","drill":"Before a peek, name the 2–3 spots they can appear from and put your crosshair on the likeliest. Hold it there.","absorbs":["4.13"],"kp":[["Pre-aim where they'll appear (crosshair already there)",["preaim","pre aim","prefire","pre fire","where they will","where theyll","where they appear","where theyre going","where they come","already on","already there","hold the spot","crosshair placement","pre place"]]]},{"id":"4.5","mod":4,"title":"Clearing corners: slice the pie, offset the crosshair","roles":["All"],"concept":"Slice the pie: clear a corner gradually with a small cone of vision and a smooth camera pan so your crosshair stays at head level. Don't glue the crosshair to the wall's edge. That assumes zero reaction time, and a fast swing blows right past it. Set it slightly off the corner in the path they'll walk, leaving a reaction-time buffer, and let them walk into it. But don't over-slice: once you've cracked someone, switch to a fast swing. Apex rewards momentum.","recall":"Why not glue your crosshair to the corner's edge, and when do you stop slicing slowly?","drill":"Holding a corner, place your crosshair a hair off the edge in their walking path. Slice unknown corners slowly; swing fast once you've cracked someone.","absorbs":["4.15"],"kp":[["Gluing it assumes zero reaction time; offset it into their path",["reaction","offset","buffer","off the corner","away from","path","blow past","swing past","too fast","overshoot","ahead"]],["Stop slicing once you've cracked someone; swing fast",["crack","momentum","damage","low","broken","knock"]]]},{"id":"4.7","mod":4,"title":"Don't move when you don't need to","roles":["All"],"concept":"If you can see and shoot them from where you are, shoot from there. Moving when you don't need to takes the pressure off them and pulls you out of the gunfight — and if they've been holding you, they hear exactly where you go next. There are only three good reasons to move: (1) you cracked them — push off the health advantage; (2) you took damage — move so they push into nothing; (3) nothing's happening / bad angle — slide sideways for a new angle while keeping your space.","recall":"Name the three — and the only three — reasons to move in a gunfight.","drill":"In a fight, if you can already shoot them, plant and shoot. Only move for one of the three reasons — catch yourself moving out of habit.","kp":[["You cracked them: push the advantage",["crack","advantage","damage them","hurt them","they are low","theyre low","broke","knock"]],["You took damage: move so they push into nothing",["took damage","got hit","got cracked","i got","hurt","took dmg","im low","i am low","low"]],["Nothing's happening or bad angle: get a new angle",["angle","nothing","stalemate","no shot","cant see","reposition","better"]]]},{"id":"4.8","mod":4,"title":"Info peek before you swing","roles":["All"],"concept":"Don't wide-swing blind into someone already holding you — you'll be hunting for them while they beam you. Take a small info peek first (a step or two) to find where they are; now your crosshair is already on them when you commit, so you pre-fire instead of “where is he — oh there” a beat too late. Position to the corner and peek out; don't start your reposition already in the open.","recall":"Why take a small info peek before committing to a swing?","drill":"Every peek: info peek to locate them, then peek to pre-fire the spot you found. Never wide-swing before you know where they are.","kp":[["Find where they are first",["where they","find them","locate","info","see them","know where","spot them","where he","where the enemy","where theyre","find where","find out"]],["So your crosshair is already on them when you commit",["crosshair","already","prefire","pre fire","preaim","pre aim","aim on","on target"]]]},{"id":"4.9","mod":4,"title":"Weapons are tools: set up, then finish","roles":["All"],"concept":"Your two guns do different jobs. Primary = your setter-upper: better range, bigger mag, more control — you use it to hold the enemy and control what they can and can't do. Secondary = your finisher: better up-close damage, hipfire, mobility — you use it to close and finish. Set up with the primary, follow through with the secondary. Doing it backwards leaves you unable to fight at the exact moment you push.","recall":"Which gun sets up the fight and which finishes it — and why?","drill":"This session, consciously control at range with your primary and finish the push with your secondary. Notice if you've been doing it backwards.","kp":[["Primary sets up: range, control, holding them",["primary","range","long","control","hold","set up","setup","marksman","sniper","poke"]],["Secondary finishes: up-close damage",["secondary","finish","close","shotgun","smg","up close","hipfire","short"]]]},{"id":"4.10","mod":4,"title":"Reload and heal on the move","roles":["All"],"concept":"Don't reload or heal before or during your push — that's when you need to be shooting. Do it while you're already moving: reload and heal off a slide, glide, or wall jump that carries your momentum. That way you're never standing still and unable to fight at the moment you're exposed.","recall":"When should you reload and heal — and when definitely not?","drill":"Tie every reload and heal to a movement (slide or glide). Catch yourself if you reload standing still mid-push.","kp":[["While moving: off a slide, glide or wall jump",["moving","move","slide","glide","wall jump","on the move","momentum","run"]],["Never before or during a push",["push","before","during","fight","exposed","in the open"]]]},{"id":"4.12","mod":4,"title":"Crosshair · head level, off the ground","roles":["All"],"concept":"Depth on 4.4. Most players drift their crosshair to the floor scanning for loot — and every fight that starts there, they're aiming at dirt while the enemy's already on their chest. Keep your crosshair pinned at head height as you move; trust your legs to carry you, you don't need to watch the ground. On controller especially this is free value — the fight starts with your reticle already where it needs to be.","recall":"Where should your crosshair rest as you move, and why do players get it wrong?","drill":"Run the range or an empty match keeping your crosshair locked at head level. Catch yourself every time it sinks to the floor.","kp":[["At head height as you move",["head","eye level","chest"]],["Players drift to the floor looking for loot",["floor","ground","loot","down","dirt"]]]},{"id":"4.14","mod":4,"title":"Crosshair · the placement paradox","roles":["All"],"concept":"Depth on 4.4. Heads-up so you don't panic: as your placement improves, your aim will temporarily feel WORSE. Your muscle memory is trained for big sweeping flicks off the floor; once the crosshair sits at head level, corrections shrink to tiny movements and you overshoot with your old force. That's not your aim breaking — it's recalibrating. Push through it, maybe nudge sensitivity down slightly, and it settles into far more consistent aim.","recall":"Why does your aim get temporarily worse when you fix your placement — and what do you do about it?","drill":"When you start overshooting after fixing placement, don't revert — keep at it a few sessions and let muscle memory recalibrate.","kp":[["Muscle memory is tuned for big flicks; corrections are tiny now, so you overshoot",["muscle","flick","overshoot","over shoot","small","tiny","used to","habit","recalibrat","adjust"]],["Push through it, maybe lower sensitivity a bit",["push through","keep going","stick with","sens","patien","practice","keep at","dont panic","temporar","settle"]]]},{"id":"4.16","mod":4,"title":"Peek-and-hide: control with cover","roles":["All"],"concept":"Peeker's advantage weaponized. Pre-aim, peek and shoot — then the millisecond they shoot back, hide behind cover. They're left shooting nothing; when they stop, you re-peek on YOUR terms and repeat. You set the cadence, they react and guess. It's the single biggest thing that stops you getting one-clipped — but only with awareness first (never blind, no info). If you build a big enough lead, then just take the kill.","recall":"Walk the peek-and-hide loop — and what must come before it for it to work?","drill":"In a cover fight: peek-shoot, hide the instant they shoot back, wait, re-peek on your terms. Make them react to your cadence.","kp":[["Pre-aim, peek and shoot",["peek","shoot","preaim","pre aim"]],["Hide the moment they shoot back, then re-peek on your terms",["hide","cover","duck","repeek","re peek","again","back"]],["Needs awareness first, never blind",["info","aware","know where","blind","first"]]]},{"id":"4.17","mod":4,"title":"Movement in the duel","roles":["All"],"concept":"Two consistency levers up close. One: swing clean and fast — strafe straight left/right (diagonal is slow and easy to hit) with your crosshair already placed, so you win the peek. Two: don't strafe predictably — the same tight repeating strafe is easy to track, and on controller a lazy repeating strafe is exactly what good players read. Vary it, use wall jumps and off-angles to break their aim. (Tap-strafing is a mouse-only tech, so on controller lean on clean strafes, wall jumps, and off-angles instead.)","recall":"What are the two movement levers that win close duels — and which famous tech isn't available to you on controller?","drill":"This session: swing straight (not diagonal) with crosshair pre-placed, and consciously vary your strafe instead of repeating one.","kp":[["Clean, fast left/right strafes (not diagonal)",["strafe","clean","fast","left","right","side to side","diagonal","straight"]],["Don't be predictable: vary it, wall jumps, off-angles",["predict","vary","random","mix","wall jump","off angle","offangle","change","pattern"]],["Tap-strafing is mouse-only",["tap"]]]},{"id":"5.1","mod":5,"title":"Be the eyes and ears","roles":["Alter","Valk"],"concept":"Good calls come from constant information. While teammates loot or fight, keep checking the map: the ring and EVO, the teams around you, your next source of info, a better spot to hold. And listen: footsteps, reloads, abilities and gunfire tell you direction, numbers and distance. A usable tell: if only one enemy is chasing you, the other is probably reviving or healing and can't shoot. Punish that.","recall":"What are you watching and listening for while your team loots or fights?","drill":"One game, call the map every 20–30 seconds and call enemy positions off audio alone (“steps left,” “reload close”).","absorbs":["5.5"],"kp":[["Watch the map: ring, teams, info, better spots",["map","ring","zone","teams","minimap","evo","position","spot","kill feed","killfeed"]],["Listen: footsteps, reloads, abilities, gunfire",["footstep","listen","sound","audio","gunfire","shots","reload","hear","steps"]]]},{"id":"5.2","mod":5,"title":"Comms: say your state, suggest, don't command","roles":["All"],"concept":"Nobody knows what you're thinking, so say it: “I need to heal,” “pushing,” “hold,” “let's leave.” When you make a call, don't command; randoms resist orders. Suggest the play, give the reason, and say why it matters: “I think we take this building. Everyone's rotating to us and it has cover, so we'll be protected when they push.” Same idea, opposite reaction. And mind the timing: don't flood comms mid-fight, and let teammates talk.","recall":"What should you always say out loud, and how do you deliver a call so randoms follow it?","drill":"Call your own state every time it changes, and make one call per game as suggest, reason, why it matters.","absorbs":["5.3"],"kp":[["Say your state: healing, pushing, holding, leaving",["state","heal","push","hold","leav","doing","intent","plan"]],["Suggest with a reason; don't command",["suggest","reason","why","explain","dont command","not command","not order","ask","because"]]]},{"id":"5.4","mod":5,"title":"Fight in sync: play off your teammates","roles":["All"],"concept":"Fights are won by the team that applies pressure together. Treat every teammate's play as your own: if they get a knock or a crack, you got it, so push off it. If they're holding a team, that team can't fight you, so step up. Fighting out of sync turns a 3v3 into a 1v3, so glance at the minimap every few seconds to check your team can actually fight with you, and call the plan before you act (“pushing on your crack, go right”), not as you're already gone.","recall":"Your teammate gets a knock across the fight: what do you do? And what keeps you in sync?","drill":"Every fight, push off your teammate's knocks and pressure, and call your plan before you move.","absorbs":["5.6"],"kp":[["Push off their knock as if it were yours",["push","follow","go in","capitaliz","play off","trade","swing"]],["Check the minimap and call the plan before you act",["minimap","mini map","map","call","comm","say","tell","plan","before"]]]},{"id":"6.4","mod":6,"title":"Why you choke (name it to tame it)","roles":["All"],"concept":"At the last two or three squads, your brain flips into fight-or-flight without asking — heart rate up, breathing shallow, fine motor control (your aim) drops, and your awareness tunnels so you stop seeing flanks and thirds. That's why you miss shots you normally hit and ego-challenge into the open. You're not bad and it's not luck — it's your body panicking at the worst time. Just knowing that's what's happening is the first step to controlling it.","recall":"What's actually happening in your body when you choke in a final fight — and why does naming it help?","drill":"Next final ring, when you feel the surge, label it: “that's adrenaline, not danger.” Naming it drains the panic's power.","kp":[["Fight-or-flight: heart rate up, shallow breathing, worse aim, tunnel vision",["flight","adrenaline","heart","breath","tunnel","panic","stress","nerves","shak","fine motor","motor"]],["Naming it is the first step to controlling it",["name","know","aware","recogni","understand","control","first step","label"]]]},{"id":"6.5","mod":6,"title":"The reset breath + next play","roles":["All"],"concept":"Two tools against the choke. One: a single steady breath before the last fight resets your aim, nerves, and decisions — sounds dumb, works. Pair it with pre-fight confidence — decide before you drop that you can win end games; your brain plays how it prepares. Two: don't dwell on mistakes mid-fight. Miss a shot or get cracked? Do NOT freeze on it — focus on the next play, not the one you just botched. Dwelling is how one mistake becomes three. Tilt between fights works the same way: reset your head before the next one, because tilt costs more RP than any single death.","recall":"What two mental tools fight the choke — the one before the fight and the one during it?","drill":"Before your next final ring: one slow breath. During: if you miss or get cracked, say “next play” and move on.","kp":[["Before: one steady breath (plus pre-fight confidence)",["breath","breathe","confiden"]],["During: don't dwell, focus on the next play",["next play","next","dwell","move on","forget","let go","mistake"]]]},{"id":"6.6","mod":6,"title":"Rehearse the chaos","roles":["All"],"concept":"Players who stay calm in final rings aren't fearless — they've seen chaos so many times it's normal. Train that on purpose: in the range or mixtape, force yourself into uncomfortable spots — 1v2s, low health, limited ammo, bad positions — so pressure stops being a shock. Stress becomes normal once you've practiced it. This is a warmup you DO, not a fact you recall.","recall":"Why do calm players stay calm under pressure — and how do you train it?","drill":"Add a chaos rep to your warmup: take a deliberate 1v2 or low-HP fight in mixtape before ranked, so real pressure feels familiar.","kp":[["They've seen chaos so often it feels normal",["normal","used to","seen","experience","familiar","exposure","often","many times"]],["Train it: practice uncomfortable spots (1v2s, low HP, bad positions)",["1v2","1v3","uncomfortable","low health","low hp","bad position","range","mixtape","practice","drill","pressure","limited ammo"]]]},{"id":"6.7","mod":6,"title":"Think in percentages; judge decisions, not results","roles":["All"],"concept":"Apex is a percentage game. You're not trying to win every match; you're trying to make the play that works most often, and over 50–100 games good decisions compound. Outcomes lie: you can win a terrible fight and lose a smart one, so judge the decision, not the result. After a fight ask one thing: was that decision reasonable? Yes, keep it. No, fix it. That's also where calm comes from: when you know why you made a call, a loss doesn't tilt you.","recall":"Why can't you judge your play by whether you won, and what do you judge it by instead?","drill":"After a loss, ask “was the decision high-percentage?” If yes, let it go. Review one fight you won badly and one you lost well.","absorbs":["6.8"],"kp":[["Outcomes lie: a bad play can win and a good one can lose",["luck","outcome","result","lie","variance","random","percentage","percent"]],["Judge the decision: was it reasonable?",["decision","reasonable","choice","process","good play","right play"]]]},{"id":"6.9","mod":6,"title":"After every death: review the chain","roles":["All"],"concept":"Every death traces to a decision 5–30 seconds earlier, so don't review the moment you got shot; review the chain. Watch your clips like a coach watching someone else's game, ego out. Ask four questions: (1) What was I trying to achieve? (2) When did my options disappear: cover, space, teammates, info, escape? (3) Was this high-percentage, or did it only work because they misplayed? (4) What information did I ignore: audio, kill feed, teammates, zone timing?","recall":"Why review the chain instead of the final moment, and what are the four questions?","drill":"After your next death, answer all four out loud. Hunt #2 hardest: find when your options disappeared.","absorbs":["6.1","6.3"],"kp":[["The real mistake is a decision 5–30 seconds earlier",["5","30","earlier","seconds","decision","chain"]],["What was I trying to achieve?",["trying","achieve","goal","intent","plan","purpose","wanted"]],["When did my options disappear?",["option","disappear","escape","when did"]],["Was it high-percentage?",["percent","odds","likely","coin","misplay"]],["What information did I ignore?",["ignore","info","audio","kill feed","killfeed","sound"]]]},{"id":"7.1","mod":7,"title":"Space is control, and the three ways to lose it","roles":["All"],"concept":"Spacing is how far you are from cover, from your teammates, and from the enemy. The team that holds the most space decides when and how the fight happens. You lose it three ways. Stacking: clumped up, so there are no trade angles, one nade hits everyone, and you block each other's sightlines. Overextending: great ground no one can follow or cover, which is isolation, not space. Giving up space too early: backing off a better position out of panic, handing them control for free.","recall":"What three distances make up spacing, and what are the three ways you lose space?","drill":"Each fight, ask “do we control the space here?” Catch the mistake you make most (stacking, overextending, or bailing early) and fix just that one.","absorbs":["7.4"],"kp":[["Distance to cover",["cover"]],["Distance to teammates",["teammate","team","squad","mates"]],["Distance to the enemy",["enemy","enemies","opponent"]],["Lose it by stacking",["stack","clump","bunch","together","too close"]],["Lose it by overextending",["overextend","over extend","too far","isolat","alone","overcommit"]],["Lose it by giving up space too early",["give up","giving up","gave up","too early","panic","back off","retreat"]]]},{"id":"7.2","mod":7,"title":"Keep reset distance and layers","roles":["All"],"concept":"Good spacing from the enemy means: if I get cracked right now, can I slide back and pop a heal before they punish me? If no, you're too close. Keep enough distance that a crack isn't a death sentence. And keep layers between you and them — terrain, a roof, a climb — so that to reach you they have to commit to an animation or cross open ground, giving you a free punish or time to reset. Distance plus layers lets you take fights at almost no risk.","recall":"What's the test for whether you're far enough from the enemy — and what do “layers” buy you?","drill":"In each fight, position so a crack doesn't kill you (slide-back-and-bat distance) and keep one layer between you and the enemy.","kp":[["If I get cracked right now, can I back off and heal before they punish me?",["crack","heal","back","slide","punish","reset","survive","cell"]],["Layers make them commit to an animation or open ground to reach you",["layer","terrain","roof","climb","animation","open ground","commit","obstacle","between"]]]},{"id":"7.3","mod":7,"title":"Read the enemy, then take space","roles":["Maggie","Ash"],"concept":"Creating space is taking ground with intent — cutting sightlines, using utility, forcing the enemy out of their comfort zone — not just moving forward. Before you push, read how they're playing: aggressive, passive, burning utility? If they're slow and not taking space, send it and seize the power position; if they're aggressive, set up first. Taking even a little ground (a hut, a bridge edge) forces them to react, and when they burn utility to answer, that's pressure and fear — they were strong side, now they feel weak.","recall":"What should you read about the enemy before you push — and what does it tell you?","drill":"Before your next push, watch the enemy for a beat: aggressive, passive, or burning utility? Push hard only when they're passive and not taking space.","kp":[["Read whether they're aggressive, passive or burning utility",["aggress","passive","util","play style","playstyle","how they","burning","abilit","slow"]],["Passive: take space now. Aggressive: set up first",["take space","seize","send","push","set up","setup","wait","take ground","power position"]]]}],"newOrder":["4.12","4.14","4.4","4.5","0.1a","1.7","1.8","0.1b","4.8","4.2","4.16","5.1","5.4","5.2","4.1","4.7","4.9","4.10","4.17","6.9","6.7","6.4","6.5","6.6","7.1","7.2","7.3"],"habits":[{"id":"1","name":"The pinch","what":"Fighting the same team as a teammate? Split to a different angle. Never stack.","mod":"3","cards":["3.3"]},{"id":"2","name":"One small play, then back up","what":"Swing, do one thing, back out, pop a cell. Push hard only off big damage.","mod":"3","cards":["3.5","3.2"]},{"id":"3","name":"Crosshair at head level","what":"Pinned at head height while you move. Pre-aim where they'll appear; don't flick.","mod":"4","cards":["4.12","4.14","4.4","4.5"]},{"id":"4","name":"Call the side","what":"Before every fight, say strong or weak side, and why. No advantage, no fight.","mod":"0","cards":["0.3","0.1"]},{"id":"5","name":"Rotate early, scan early","what":"Move with a reason before the ring forces you. Scan the ring as early as you can.","mod":"1","cards":["1.1","1.7","1.8","0.1b"]},{"id":"6","name":"Info peek before you swing","what":"First peek finds them. Second peek fires. Never wide-swing blind.","mod":"4","cards":["4.8","4.2","4.16"]},{"id":"7","name":"Check behind while healing","what":"Swivel on every heal and every loot. Clear your back before you commit.","mod":"2","cards":["2.3","5.1"]},{"id":"8","name":"Fight in sync","what":"Push off your teammate's knocks. Call the plan before you move.","mod":"5","cards":["5.4","5.2"]}],"extraFoci":{"U":{"name":"Use your kit and grenades","what":"Tactical or a nade before every push. Throw nades before you go down."},"D":{"name":"Land with a plan","what":"Pick your gun spot and your exit before you jump."}},"ranks":["Rookie","Bronze IV","Bronze III","Bronze II","Bronze I","Silver IV","Silver III","Silver II","Silver I","Gold IV","Gold III","Gold II","Gold I","Platinum IV","Platinum III","Platinum II","Platinum I","Diamond IV","Diamond III","Diamond II","Diamond I","Master","Predator"],"legends":["Alter","Ash","Axle","Ballistic","Bangalore","Bloodhound","Catalyst","Caustic","Conduit","Crypto","Fuse","Gibraltar","Horizon","Lifeline","Loba","Mad Maggie","Mirage","Newcastle","Octane","Pathfinder","Rampart","Revenant","Seer","Sparrow","Valkyrie","Vantage","Wattson","Wraith"],"legendAliases":{"Maggie":"Mad Maggie","Valk":"Valkyrie"},"legendJobs":{"Alter":"Flex. Recon for decisions, portal as an escape hatch. Strong for rotating and third-partying.","Valkyrie":"Flex. Long rotations, scan info, and an ult that relocates the whole squad.","Conduit":"Zone. Aggressive support: keep the squad healthy and stabilize the weak side.","Catalyst":"Zone. Walls and door control turn ordinary buildings into power positions.","Mad Maggie":"Edge. Breaks holds and forces fights.","Ash":"Edge. Entry, snare and portal. Good for practicing swinging first."},"kit":{"Alter":"Void Passage open as an exit before you commit. Void Nexus to pull the team out.","Conduit":"Radiant Transfer to shield a teammate mid-fight. Energy Barricade to stall a push.","Catalyst":"Piercing Spikes to slow a push or hold a door. Dark Veil to block sight while you reset.","Mad Maggie":"Riot Drill to flush someone off cover. Wrecking Ball to break a hold.","Valkyrie":"Missile Swarm to stun before you swing. Skyward Dive to rotate or escape.","Ash":"Arc Snare to lock a target. Phase Breach to engage or escape."},"debriefCauses":{"ring":{"label":"Caught by ring","color":"var(--blue)","mod":"1"},"thirdparty":{"label":"Third party","color":"var(--amber)","mod":"2"},"push":{"label":"Bad push","color":"var(--red)","mod":"3"},"gunfight":{"label":"Lost gunfight","color":"var(--violet)","mod":"4"},"other":{"label":"Other","color":"var(--grey)","mod":null}},"maps":[{"id":"bm","name":"Broken Moon","ranked":true,"sig":"Signature: the Zip Rail network — the fastest cross-map rotation in the game.","rows":[["Structure","Moon terrain strung with <b>zip rails</b> (the lines threading the whole map) around a central void crater. Rails cover huge distance fast, but riding one leaves you <b>exposed, committed, and predictable</b> — the lobby sees you and knows where you'll land."],["Choke points","The <b>rail landings</b> are the real chokes — teams camp the exit to catch riders. The crater funnels movement around it."],["Strong spots","Elevated buildings <b>near a rail but not on the landing</b> — height and hard cover with a fast escape line if it goes wrong."],["Heavy vs quiet","Rails pull the lobby along predictable lines. The quiet side is the terrain <b>between</b> rails that teams skip. Rail for tempo, terrain to stay unseen."],["Legend fits","<b>Alter</b> and <b>Valk</b> are absurd here — rail + TP/ult means you rotate anywhere and bail a bad landing. <b>Catalyst</b> locks a rail-side building and denies the landing. <b>Maggie / Ash</b> rail into a fight and swing."],["Current read","<b>In the ranked rotation now</b> (as of Oct 2026). Low-contest climber drops: Cultivation's edge, Eternal Gardens. Rotation tool: zip rails. Verify live — drops and zones shift each split.","meta"],["Next game","Every rotation, decide “rail or terrain?” Never ride into an unknown landing without a plan for who's waiting.","focus"]]},{"id":"olym","name":"Olympus","ranked":true,"sig":"Signature: open ground. A flat, floating city — the gaps between POIs are killing fields.","rows":[["Structure","Roughly circular, POIs ringed around a center, clouds = the edge (you can fall off). Low natural height, so <b>“height” means rooftops</b>, not ridges. The <b>open fields between POIs</b> are where you get thirded crossing exposed. Phase Runner and tridents are your skip-the-open-ground tools."],["Choke points","The open fields act as exposure traps rather than true chokes. Phase Runner / the Rift are fast lanes; the paths between adjacent POIs pinch."],["Strong spots","Buildings with <b>rooftop access and multiple entrances</b>. Interior holds where you control the doors."],["Heavy vs quiet","The whole map is a “cross the open or go around” puzzle. Quiet play = hug POI edges, use tridents / Phase Runner, never sprint the open middle."],["Legend fits","<b>Catalyst</b> is elite here — buildings + doors + walls make un-pushable par positions and shut down open-ground pushes. <b>Conduit</b> stabilizes the crossings. <b>Valk</b> ult skips the open entirely. <b>Maggie / Ash</b> trident in and force a building fight."],["Current read","<b>In the ranked rotation now</b> (as of Oct 2026). Long sightlines punish late rotates hard. Safer low-contest drops: Gardens, Hydroponics, Bonsai Plaza, Docks. Hot drop (skip for RP): Hammond Labs. Central Energy Depot is closed-off — fights on steps and slopes. A building is only safe if you arrived before other squads aimed at it. Verify live.","meta"],["Next game","Stop sprinting across fields. Plan every crossing with cover, a trident, or Phase Runner. This is the cover-and-spacing map.","focus"]]},{"id":"we","name":"World's Edge","ranked":true,"sig":"Signature: lava barriers + strong height + a third-party meat grinder in the middle.","rows":[["Structure","High-contrast terrain — snow NW, lava SE, industrial center. The <b>lava veins are walls you can't cross</b>, so they channel movement into predictable lanes and crossings. Big open basins with strong ridge/building height around them. The central split POI is classic hot-drop chaos."],["Choke points","Lava fissures funnel rotations to their crossing points. The center corridor splits the map E/W and is the heavy line everyone funnels through."],["Strong spots","<b>Ridge and building height overlooking a basin</b> — height + cover over the zone is the textbook par position here."],["Heavy vs quiet","The center is the heavy side almost every game. The NW snow and SE past the lava are quieter. <b>Rotate the ring's edge, not through the middle.</b>"],["Legend fits","<b>Catalyst</b> locks a height/building par position over zone. <b>Maggie / Ash</b> farm the center chaos if you want a fight-heavy game. <b>Alter / Valk</b> take the quiet edge and third the center from height."],["Current read","<b>In the ranked rotation now</b> (as of Oct 2026). Controller legends (Catalyst) shine holding Fragment's many buildings. Safer drops: Skyhook, The Geyser; hot: Fragment East. Zone tendency: pulls mostly south — Thermal between Siphon and Harvester, or Dome; less common are Overlook, Geyser, Lava Fissure, and back-Skyhook endings. Verify live.","meta"],["Next game","Consciously route around the center. Claim your zone-side height early, and expect third parties — this map is full of them.","focus"]]},{"id":"sp","name":"Storm Point","ranked":false,"sig":"Signature: massive scale + water + gravity cannons. The biggest map — long rotations punish lateness hard.","rows":[["Structure","Landmasses separated by <b>water channels</b>. Water is open, slow, and lethal to cross on foot under pressure. <b>Land bridges</b> between masses are hard chokes. <b>Gravity cannons</b> launch you across the map — the signature rotation tool. Its size means getting caught mid-rotation is brutal."],["Choke points","The <b>land bridges</b> connecting landmasses, narrow passes between water, and gravity-cannon landing zones (camped)."],["Strong spots","Elevated landmass interiors with few water-side approaches; buildings that <b>cover a land bridge</b> — hold the bridge, control who crosses."],["Heavy vs quiet","Size spreads the lobby thin, but zone pull re-concentrates it on the central bridges everyone must cross. Quiet = outer coast and skipped landmasses."],["Legend fits","<b>Valk</b> is premium — long rotations and an ult that crosses water and distance. Her best map. <b>Alter</b> TP escapes bad crossings. <b>Catalyst</b> holds a landmass building. <b>Maggie / Ash</b> need cannons to reach fights — walking is too slow."],["Current read","<b>NOT in the ranked rotation right now</b> (as of Oct 2026 it's Broken Moon / World's Edge / Olympus) — it cycles back later, so lower priority. Edge drops are plentiful: Cascade Falls, North Pad, The Mill. Avoid the center (Command Center). Zone tendency: pulls center or the SE quadrant; few endings around Zeus, Cenote Cave, or Checkpoint. Verify live when it returns.","meta"],["Next game","Rotate EARLY — Storm Point punishes late rotations more than any map. Never cross open water on foot under pressure.","focus"]]},{"id":"ed","name":"E-District","ranked":false,"sig":"Signature: dense urban night map — compact, vertical, cover everywhere.","rows":[["Structure","Tightly packed buildings and streets with heavy verticality. Compact = <b>short rotations, constant proximity, relentless third parties</b>. Sightlines are short; fights are close-to-mid. So much cover that <b>peeking and positioning (Module 4) decide fights</b>, not long-range poke."],["Choke points","The <b>streets and alleys</b> between building clusters are the funnels where teams collide. Single-entrance interiors are traps or forts depending who holds."],["Strong spots","Buildings with <b>rooftop + multiple floors + several exits</b>. Control the verticality of a building and you own the fight."],["Heavy vs quiet","Everywhere is close on this map. “Quiet” = moving <b>through structures</b> to break line of sight instead of down open streets."],["Legend fits","<b>Catalyst</b> may be best-in-slot — doors + walls in a building-dense map make forts nobody cracks. <b>Conduit</b> stabilizes the constant close fights. <b>Maggie</b> is nasty in interiors — drill and wrecking ball force teams out of holds. <b>Alter</b> Q for surprise interior angles."],["Current read","Good drops: Draft Point (central, quick fights + crafter), the Lotus towers (north, best pure loot), Blossom Drive / Heights (same layout — learn one, know both). Avoid Energy Bank and Galleria — center death traps with constricted chokes. Strongest hold: the “door-fortress” apartment buildings (second floor, only two doorways — a Catalyst dream). Verify live.","meta"],["Next game","The Module 4 map: hitbox discipline, re-peeking, pinch angles. Hold buildings, avoid open streets, and clear your back — thirds are constant.","focus"]]},{"id":"kc","name":"Kings Canyon","ranked":false,"sig":"Signature: small, aggressive, a water channel splitting the middle. Forced fights.","rows":[["Structure","A <b>water channel cuts the map</b> (Cascades → Bridges → south), with <b>Bridges</b> as a literal named choke in the center. Small map = short rotations, more forced contact, less room to dodge fights. The central POIs are high-traffic; the edges (Airbase, Swamps, Artillery, Water Treatment) are quieter."],["Choke points","<b>Bridges</b> above all — hold it, control central rotation. The water-channel crossings and the passes between central POIs."],["Strong spots","Height over Bridges and the channel; strong building POIs (Bunker, Hydro Dam, Repulsor). A choke crossing is a natural Catalyst lock."],["Heavy vs quiet","The center is a meat grinder; the edges are quieter. On a small map the heavy side is unavoidable longer than on big maps — <b>pick your fights deliberately.</b>"],["Legend fits","<b>Maggie / Ash</b> thrive on a small aggro map — constant fights, easy KP. <b>Catalyst</b> holds a choke or building. <b>Alter / Valk</b> run the quiet edge and third the center. <b>Conduit</b> keeps you healthy through the contact."],["Note","Older map images show the classic layout (Skull Town, Thunderdome, Artillery). Live Kings Canyon has been reworked over the seasons, so some POI names and exact spots differ — but the structure (central channel + Bridges choke + small = forced fights) still holds.","warn"],["Next game","Decide before you drop: farm the center (aggro) or play the quiet edge to zone. Small map, so commit — you can't do both.","focus"]]}],"mapsNote":"Read the geometry, not the tier list. Water, lava, bridges, zip rails, height and open ground decide rotations, and none of it changes when the meta shifts. The teal Current read rows are a snapshot from Oct 2026 and will drift. For live zone endings, use the map atlas on apexlegendsstatus.com.","clips":{"types":[["death","Death"],["lostfight","Lost fight"],["knock","Knock, no kill"],["win","Win / clean fight"]],"situations":[["hotdrop","Hot drop"],["looting","Looting"],["rotating","Rotating"],["holding","Holding a spot"],["endgame","Endgame (ring 4+)"],["short","Down a teammate"]],"starters":[["us","We did"],["them","They did"],["third","A third team"],["none","No fight"]],"plans":[["fight","Take a fight"],["rotate","Rotate"],["loot","Loot"],["reset","Heal and reset"],["thirdparty","Third-party someone"],["hold","Hold a spot"],["noplan","No real plan"]],"when":[["lt5","Under 5s before"],["5to30","5–30s before"],["30to60","30–60s before"],["ring","A ring earlier"]],"kinds":[["decision","Decision"],["execution","Execution"],["both","Both"],["neither","Neither"]],"groups":{"drop":{"title":"The drop","checks":[{"k":"dropplan","t":"I landed with a plan: where my gun is and where we exit","f":"D"},{"k":"dropnear","t":"I landed close enough to help my teammates","f":"D"},{"k":"dropgun","t":"I had a gun before I took a fight","f":"D"},{"k":"dropleave","t":"We left when the drop went bad, instead of staying to die","f":"D"}]},"position":{"title":"Position and rotation","checks":[{"k":"early","t":"We moved before the ring forced us","f":"5"},{"k":"cover","t":"Our path had cover","f":"5"},{"k":"side","t":"We knew if we were on the strong or weak side","f":"4"},{"k":"height","t":"I kept height, and didn't drop off it just because a teammate did","f":"4","hint":"Follow the plan, not the person. Dropping off height gives away your advantage."},{"k":"count","t":"I knew how many enemies we were fighting","f":"6"},{"k":"ready","t":"I was healed, shielded and reloaded before it started","f":"2"},{"k":"behind","t":"Someone checked behind us for a third party","f":"7"},{"k":"split","t":"I took a different angle than my teammate (no stacking)","f":"1"}]},"comms":{"title":"Comms","checks":[{"k":"saw","t":"I called what I saw: where, how many, how hurt","f":"8"},{"k":"doing","t":"I said what I was doing before I did it","f":"8","hint":"\"Swinging left.\" \"Healing.\" \"Resetting to the rock.\""},{"k":"ping","t":"I pinged enemies instead of only saying them","f":"8"},{"k":"knew","t":"My teammates knew where I was","f":"8"},{"k":"calls","t":"Calls were made, and we acted on them","f":"8"},{"k":"plancall","t":"Someone called the plan before we moved","f":"8"}]},"fight":{"title":"Gunfight","checks":[{"k":"head","t":"My crosshair stayed at head height","f":"3"},{"k":"preaim","t":"I pre-aimed where they came out","f":"3"},{"k":"peek","t":"I info-peeked before I swung","f":"6"},{"k":"coverpeek","t":"I shot from cover and didn't stand in the open","f":"6"},{"k":"small","t":"I made one small play, then backed up","f":"2"},{"k":"bigdmg","t":"I only pushed hard off big damage or a knock","f":"2"},{"k":"reload","t":"I reloaded and healed behind cover","f":"7"},{"k":"sync","t":"We pushed together off a teammate's knock","f":"8"}]},"kit":{"title":"Kit and grenades","checks":[{"k":"tac","t":"I used my tactical in this fight","f":"U","legendHint":true},{"k":"ult","t":"I used my ultimate, or had a reason to save it","f":"U","legendHint":true},{"k":"nades","t":"I threw my grenades before I went down","f":"U","hint":"Tap N/A if you had none."},{"k":"utilfirst","t":"I used utility before pushing, not after","f":"U"}]}},"causes":[{"k":"ring","label":"Caught by ring","f":"5","color":"var(--blue)"},{"k":"thirdparty","label":"Third party","f":"7","color":"var(--amber)"},{"k":"push","label":"Bad push","f":"2","color":"var(--red)"},{"k":"gunfight","label":"Lost gunfight","f":"3","color":"var(--violet)"},{"k":"comms","label":"Team out of sync","f":"8","color":"var(--teal)"},{"k":"spacing","label":"Stacked / gave up height","f":"1","color":"var(--sand)"},{"k":"util","label":"Didn't use my kit","f":"U","color":"var(--rose)"},{"k":"drop","label":"Lost the hot drop","f":"D","color":"var(--olive)"},{"k":"other","label":"Something else","f":null,"color":"var(--grey)"},{"k":"win","label":"Clean play to repeat","f":null,"color":"var(--teal)"}],"recorderNote":"Your clips run about 2 minutes either side of the event if your recorder is set that way (Outplayed, Medal, ShadowPlay). Open one, then follow the steps here."}};
